Id: upd002w
Severity: WARN
Category: packages
Check: check_updates
References: unattended-upgrades(8), dnf-automatic(8)
Controls: CIS 7.3, 7.4; NIST SI-2; ISO 8.8; CE Security update management

Security updates are not installed automatically, so each stays
uninstalled until someone remembers to install it. Most of the
machines broken into through a published hole were unpatched for
weeks after the fix was out. Installing security updates (not all
updates) automatically is safe enough on most systems, and the
distributions ship the means.

To fix it, on Debian and Ubuntu install unattended-upgrades and turn
it on ('dpkg-reconfigure -plow unattended-upgrades'; by default it
installs only security updates); on Linux Mint, turn on automatic
updates in Update Manager's preferences. On Fedora and RHEL, install
dnf-automatic (dnf5-plugin-automatic on recent Fedora), set
apply_updates = yes and upgrade_type = security in
/etc/dnf/automatic.conf, and enable dnf-automatic.timer. Where updates
are deployed another way (configuration management, an image
pipeline), accept the finding with tigris-accept.
